The following is a comprehensive transcript of the first televised leaders' debate of the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|61st General Election]], hosted by [[KTK News]] at their Okami headquarters on April 30, 2026.

== Background ==
{{main|Okaiken general election, 2026|March Maelstrom|En Crisis}}

The debate occurred seventeen days before the May 17 election, at a moment of profound political crisis in Okaiken. The incumbent [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] government of Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] had collapsed following the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] and the subsequent "March Maelstrom" protests. The [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]], formerly a coalition partner, had withdrawn from government, leaving Honda to lead a minority administration. The atmosphere was described by the ''[[Okami Times]]'' as "combustible."

=== The March Maelstrom ===
{{main|March Maelstrom|Nakayama Station dragging incident|Mirror Yoshi}}
The March Maelstrom refers to the sustained civil unrest that began on March 14, 2026, following the death of 17-year-old high school student {{nihongo|Yamamoto Hina|山本日奈}} at [[Nakayama Station]] in [[Okayama Prefecture]]. The student's bag became caught in an automated train door; the train departed, dragging her approximately 340 meters along the platform before the emergency stop was engaged. She died from her injuries en route to Mito Central Hospital.

The incident sparked immediate outrage when it was revealed that the station had been operating under "one-man automated protocols" as part of the Honda government's Digital Rail Initiative. The {{nihongo|'''Mirror Yoshi'''|鏡よし|Kagami Yoshi}} movement—named for the reflective safety vests worn by railway workers—emerged within 48 hours, demanding a complete halt to single-operator automated rail services and the restoration of two-person crews on all OR lines.

By the time of the April 30 debate, the protests had spread to all 48 prefectures, with major demonstrations occurring daily at [[Okami Central Station]], [[Toyozawa Station]], and [[Aizawa Station]]. The OR Workers Union had initiated a general strike that had paralyzed 40% of the Harujima Shinkansen service and 65% of conventional rail lines.

=== The En Crisis ===
{{main|En Crisis}}
The Sanese En (¥) had lost 34% of its value against the Volisanian mark (ℳ) since January 2026, reaching an all-time low of ¥247 = ℳ1 on April 28. The crisis was precipitated by a combination of factors:

* '''Algorithmic trading failures''': The Satori AI system, which managed 60% of all En-denominated transactions, experienced a cascade failure on February 19, triggering a sell-off that the Bank of Okaiken was unable to arrest.
* '''Loss of confidence in digital infrastructure''': International investors questioned the stability of Okaiken's "digital-first" currency policy, which had eliminated physical currency reserves in favor of blockchain-backed digital assets.
* '''Contagion from the Kizan Systems scandal''': The revelation that the semi-state semiconductor manufacturer had falsified three years of financial data undermined confidence in the broader Sanese tech sector.

Inflation had reached 18.7% by April 2026, the highest level since the [EVENT]. Food prices had increased 42% year-over-year, with rice—the staple of the Sanese diet—reaching ¥890 per 5kg bag, up from ¥520 in January.

